@@s001 Catalogue of hard problems
@@s002 dato:known problem (P2),ora:exam problem (P1),ok:reduction verified
@@s003 The catalogue: NP-complete decision problems. For the proof choose a known P2 with the same structure as your P1.
@@s004 <b>Independent Set ≡ Vertex Cover</b>: G has an independent set of size k if and only if the complement is a vertex cover of size n − k. Equivalent.
@@s005 <b>Set Cover</b> generalises VC (VC is the case where each set has two elements). <b>3SAT → Independent Set</b> with a gadget. Each arrow is a polynomial reduction, in the direction from the known to the new.
@@s006 Catalogue of hard problems
@@s007 The NP-complete problems to use as P2 in the reductions. Choose the one with the structure most similar to your problem: independent set, cover, path through all the nodes, subset sum, colouring.
@@s008 The catalogue
@@s009 Decision problems, recognised from the wording of the text:
@@s010 <b>Independent Set</b> (k nodes pairwise non-adjacent): "no two adjacent", "never at both ends of a wire".
@@s011 <b>Vertex Cover</b> (k nodes touching every edge): "every wire monitored", "buy all tiles".
@@s012 <b>Set Cover</b> (k sets whose union is everything): "fewest … whose union is everything".
@@s013 <b>3SAT</b> (clauses of three literals): "one of three wishes".
@@s014 <b>Hamiltonian cycle / path</b> (cycle or path touching all the nodes): "simple path", "all".
@@s015 <b>3-coloring</b>, <b>Clique</b>, <b>Subset sum / Partition</b> (numbers to split exactly in two).
@@s016 How to recognise the problem from the text
@@s017 The special-case test
@@s018 Before building the reduction, check that your P1 contains P2 as a special case: fix a parameter of P1 at the extreme value (<code>k = 2</code>, <code>r = n</code>, <code>W = V</code>, <code>t = 0</code>) and check whether you get exactly P2. It is the fastest way to choose the right P2.
@@s019 The chain of reductions
@@s020 From 3-SAT to everything else (the scheme of the slides)
@@s021 The easy look-alikes
@@s022 Every hard problem has a <b>twin</b> that looks the same but is solved in polynomial time. What changes is a detail: a cycle that passes through every <i>node</i> is hard, a cycle that passes through every <i>edge</i> is easy.
