@@s053 If the numbers of the problem are tiny and the problem "looks like a cover", the text leaves room for exhaustive search. At the exam, however, the structure decides, not the numbers: you must be able to say which known problem to reduce from.
@@s054 Reductions already seen in the exams
@@s055 In past exams the reduction is almost the identity: each piece of the known instance is translated into a piece of the new problem. No complicated gadgets are needed as in 3-SAT. The work is recognising the known problem hidden in the text.
