@@s099 ok:just added to the chain
@@s100 One starts from <b>3-SAT</b>, NP-complete by the Cook-Levin theorem. Each arrow A → B is a reduction A ≤<sub>p</sub> B: B is at least as hard as A.
@@s101 <b>Packing and covering</b>: 3-SAT → Independent Set (triangle gadget) → Vertex Cover (complement) → Set Cover (special case).
@@s102 <b>Sequencing</b>: 3-SAT → directed Hamiltonian Cycle → undirected → TSP and Longest Path.
@@s103 <b>Partitioning</b>: 3-SAT → 3-Color (colouring a graph with 3 colours). <b>Numerical</b>: 3-SAT → Subset Sum → Knapsack (in the book Subset Sum goes through 3D-Matching).
@@s104 At the exam one does not redo the whole chain: one takes the problem <b>closest</b> to the new one (the one from which the reduction is shortest) and reduces from it.
